{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ Eviction Law grounds — criminal offenses and assault","requirement":"A landlord may file for eviction when the tenant is convicted of, pleads guilty to, or a juvenile is adjudicated delinquent for assault or terrorist threats against the landlord, family or employees; civil liability for theft, assault or drug activity on the premises; theft of property; or human trafficking.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in New Jersey where the tenant, or a person the tenant knowingly permits to reside there, has the specified conviction or civil finding.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"residential rental premises"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three days prior to filing suit for eviction (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(q)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ owner-occupied two- and three-family eviction causes and notice","requirement":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings may be removed only by court order, and only for the listed causes — holdover, nonpayment, disorderly conduct, willful damage, or violating rules or lease terms — with the stated notice to quit.","key_value":"3-month / 1-month / 3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings in New Jersey.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A three-day written Notice to Quit is required for any of the causes described as disorderly, destructive or violative of written rules or lease provisions (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ eviction-law violation penalties (owner occupancy and removal from residential use)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts a tenant to personally occupy the unit must occupy it at least six months; a landlord who evicts to board up, demolish or permanently retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar; up to $10,000 civil penalty","coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units vacated after notice that the landlord will personally occupy the unit or will permanently remove the property from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"owner-occupancy and demolition/retirement evictions"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ anti-reprisal protection for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not take reprisal action by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises protected civil rights, such as enforcing lease or law rights or making a good-faith complaint to a governmental authority.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"all residential rentals including mobile homes"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Eligible senior, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled-veteran tenants in buildings being converted may be protected from eviction for 40 years if family income is within limits, and the converter must notify tenants of the right to file.","key_value":"40 years' protected tenancy","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or qualifying disabled veterans, who lived in the building being converted at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy","requirement":"The Act extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties, currently only Hudson County, in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act.","key_value":"Hudson County only","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties — currently only Hudson County — in buildings converted or being converted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"conversions in Hudson County"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Rooming and boarding houses — buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms — subject to the Rooming and Boarding House Act exceptions.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students, and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"NJ tenant protections in foreclosure","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; a tenant may be evicted for a buyer's personal occupancy only after a 90-day notice to quit, and any buyer of a foreclosed property with residential rental units must notify tenants within 10 business days that ownership changed and they need not move.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10-business-day notice","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed properties containing one or more residential rental units; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"foreclosed residential rental property"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Any person acquiring a foreclosed property containing one or more residential rental units must provide notices to the tenants in English and Spanish, within 10 business days after the sale, letting tenants know that ownership has changed hands and that the tenants are not required to move because of the foreclosure.","interaction":"Federal foreclosure law does not preempt State or local law that gives tenants longer periods or additional protections.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
